<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palazzo Vecchio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1299">1299</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Palazzo della Signoria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palazzo Vecchio is the town hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, built from 1299 as the seat of the Signoria, probably to designs by Arnolfo di Cambio. Its fortress-like mass and tall tower dominate the Piazza della Signoria. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a> lived there before moving to the Palazzo Pitti, and its interiors were redecorated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a>; it has again been Florence's city hall since 1872.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palazzo-vecchio.jpg" alt="Palazzo Vecchio" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Bini · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piazza_della_signoria,_palazzo_vecchio,_veduta_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
